{"authored" [{"question" "e-11507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the missing limits in the repeated integral below\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V \n= \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \nf(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type III region defined as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x \\leq z \\leq 2x+2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq z+xz \\right\\}.\n$$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-11507/review.json" "file_sha256" "71773c97af4b02de4a8b8bb816519c07c7bc60d98cd152ca5c735bf864606762" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-11507/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "3e065b95c173155ed8c48ec5455c26f440f027b459df6388dfc60ae586d6c4b9" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-11507/verification.json" "basis" 2081 "rationale" "Preserve exact R; remove reversed/empty fibers using three actual base branches."} {"question" "e-11507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Retain the defining inequalities of $R$ exactly. Ordered $z$ bounds require $x\\le2x+2$, so $x\\ge-2$. A nonempty $y$ fiber requires $(1+x)z\\ge0$. For $-2\\le x\\le-1$, both $1+x$ and every allowed $z$ are nonpositive. For $-1\\le x\\le0$, keep only $z\\ge0$. For $0\\le x\\le3$, the printed lower bound $z=x$ is already nonnegative. Therefore\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\iiint_R f\\,dV={}&\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\int_x^{2x+2}\\int_0^{z+xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\\\\&+\\int_{-1}^{0}\\int_0^{2x+2}\\int_0^{z+xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\\\\&+\\int_0^{3}\\int_x^{2x+2}\\int_0^{z+xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThese three integrals cover the actual region with ordered bounds; boundary overlap has measure zero. For $f=1$, their volumes are $5/24$, $1/2$, and $903/8$, totaling $1363/12$. The source solution used $x\\in[-3,3]$ and $z\\in[x,2x+2]$ throughout, including reversed or empty fibers; its formal signed integral gives $111$ and does not integrate over $R$." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-11507/review.json" "file_sha256" "71773c97af4b02de4a8b8bb816519c07c7bc60d98cd152ca5c735bf864606762" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-11507/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "3e065b95c173155ed8c48ec5455c26f440f027b459df6388dfc60ae586d6c4b9" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/e-11507/verification.json" "basis" 2081 "rationale" "Preserve exact R; remove reversed/empty fibers using three actual base branches."} {"question" "e-11507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the missing limits in the repeated integral below\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V \n= \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \n\\int_{\\ast}^{\\ast} \nf(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\: \\mathrm{d}z \\: \\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the type III region defined as\n\n$$\nR=\\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x \\leq z \\leq 2x+2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq z+xz \\right\\}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063571/example-11507.json" "file_sha256" "986ae39d074de46d4c8c045618b2e00fc535197a903dcb321617ce6190d200af" "basis" 2077 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063571/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-11507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z)  \\: : \\: (x,z) \\in D, \\: u_1(x,z) \\leq y \\leq u_2(x,z) \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063571/assets/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d V = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[\\int_{u_1(x,z)}^{u_2(x,z)} f(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d y \\right] \\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu_1(x,z) = 0, \\qquad u_2(x,z)  = z+xz.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint \\limits_{S} f(x,y,z) \\: \\textrm{d}V \n= \n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\left[ \\int_{0}^{z+xz} f(x,y,z) \\: \\mathrm{d}y \\right] \\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD =\\left\\{ (x,z) \\: : \\: -3 \\leq x \\leq 3, \\: x \\leq z \\leq 2x+2 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{z+xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{x}^{2x+2}[{\\int }_{0}^{z+xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{x}^{2x+2}{\\int }_{0}^{z+xz}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063571/example-11507.json" "file_sha256" "986ae39d074de46d4c8c045618b2e00fc535197a903dcb321617ce6190d200af" "basis" 2077 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063571/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [] "usage" {"e-11507" []} "basis" 2081 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
